well the housing and community secretary  robert generic is with us this morning  good morning to you thank you for  joining us  how did you decide as a government which  tears people would be in  well the scientists advised us that  there were five  tests they include things like the rate  of infection in any particular area  whether it's rising or falling the rate  amongst the over 60s the people who are  most likely to get  seriously unwell and what's going on in  local hospitals  and using those five tests we looked at  all the different local authority areas  in the country and came to a balanced  judgment for each one as to where they  should sit  within the tiers and that's what the  prime minister set out now  which of course subject to the voting  parliament will be coming into effect  next week  how um do places exit lockdown that  we're in a higher  tier thinking particularly of say  liverpool well the the tiered approach  that we had before the new national  measures  was working i think most people would  agree it's the right approach  to take a localized proportionate way  forward rather than trying to have  blanket  lockdowns across the whole of england  but we did learn from it  that the tiers were probably not strong  enough to have the desired effect so  tier one  was having some effect it was slowing  the rate of infection  but it wasn't really bringing it under  control tier two  was flattening the rate of infection but  in many parts of the country that wasn't  clearly bringing it down  and tier three was having an effect in  most parts of the country although not  all but not as much as one might have  wanted to see  so i think if you want the tiered  approach to continue which we certainly  do  you need to be sure that it's going to  work and that it will work in most parts  of the country and so  following the advice of sage and the  chief medical officer  and his team we've designed the tears to  be slightly different to the way they  were  before so that we can have much greater  confidence  that they will succeed and that the  battle should be about  de-escalating from those tears rather  than playing catch-up  and seeing places rise up the tears in  the future  so why can people in tier three i've  just looked i was looking in the paper  this morning  people in tier three can drop their kids  off at school go to primark  do some christmas shopping have a  workout get a haircut  go for a swim but not go to a restaurant  what's the logic behind that well  because these are these are judgments  aren't they  well the judgment call is that we want  to prioritize education  and so taking your kids to school  ensuring that they can stay in  face-to-face  education it's just incredibly important  to us all as parents  as a society we want to ensure that  people can stay and work  although if they can work from home they  are strongly advised to do so  but if you can't as many people clearly  can't if you work in manufacturing  agriculture  food processing then you should and can  continue to go to work  we want to ensure people can get the  essentials of life by going out to  to the shops we think hair and beauty is  very important to people's  mental health and general well-being i  think we'd all agree with that  so there are difficult choices therefore  as to where we can reduce the rate of  infection  and some of those settings particularly  indoors  where you are meeting people in a social  setting  are the sorts of places one would look  sadly to try to  retrench and reduce the rate of  infection that leads us  to the hospitality sector none of us do  this  you know with anything but a heavy heart  because we know how difficult it's been  for all those local business people in  our own areas and neighborhoods  over the course of this year but it is a  way  of reducing the rate of infection  reducing the social contact that we have  with others and therefore protecting  ourselves and the rider community  uh so extra funding then for tiers two  and three  there is funding for them so for the  businesses they have the business grants  the chancellor has announced  in the past we've provided every local  council with  funding for discretionary business  support schemes  and many of those supports have only  been launched in the last two or three  weeks and so there's  enough money available for at least the  coming months to support businesses  the furlough scheme of course continues  to the end of the financial year  by which time let's all hope that we're  in a dramatically  better position that's when we're  approaching easter when the cmo and  others think that  uh the vaccine rollout should really  have taken effect and we can be looking  to  move into better times and then for  local councils which i'm responsible for  there's also further support for them so  we're providing them with  more money so they can invest in local  community testing  targeted testing in particular settings  like care homes  yeah mass testing is all important as we  saw with liverpool which is why it came  down  to all intents and purposes from level  three to uh level two  um how do you decide who gets that  does every local authority have an  opportunity do you have to be in tier  three do you need to apply  if you're andy burnham are you at the  back of the queue no absolutely not ev  every  every local authority is being given  lateral flow tests  to do targeted testing that's as i say  testing say  particular workplaces where there might  be a particular risk  care home schools universities but those  places that are in tier three  now have the opportunity to do the same  as liverpool did which is to do  mass community testing and so we're  asking any local authority in those  places to come to us  uh say if they'd like to be part of that  and then we will provide them  not just with the tests but with the  expertise the support the things we've  learned from  the liverpool experience which as you  say was very successful and with  military support  as well for the logistics and if all of  the country that's in tier 3 wanted to  do it  then we would work on a priority basis  but certainly the councils that have  approached me so far  kirk lees for example in west yorkshire  who said they'd be very interested  we're working with them now at pace to  try to get them the resources  and make a success of it i think it was  one of the sort of arrows in the quiver  of liverpool's success they used this  very effectively  we'd like to see the same happening in  other parts of the country um  how quickly can people come out of their  tears it depends who you talk to  is there a possibility let's use greater  manchester as an example that they can  come out of tier three into tier two  before christmas  it certainly is possible there will be a  review point in  14 days time around the 16th of december  so at that point we advised by the  experts we'll  look at each local authority area in the  country and see whether they should  remain in their existing till where  there's a potential  uh to move down the tiers there are a  number of places which were quite finely  balanced  judgments where they were on the cusp of  different tiers so those are the places  which are perhaps more likely to be in  that position  but we've also got to bear in mind see  some movement then do you i think it's  possible but  to finish my point we've also got to  bear in mind that there will be an  opening  over the christmas period which is  likely to drive some  higher rate of infection as some people  choose to go and meet family and friends  uh for christmas day and the days  surrounding it and so you we have to  bear that in mind  our overall approach is trying to ensure  that the tears  hold the line and that places are in a  process of de-escalation what we don't  want to do is ease up too quickly  and then find that in january we're  having to uh to put tears back in place  again but there's every reason to  believe that uh places could see a  change at  the 16th 17th of december mark of course  you have to get  all of this through parliament and there  are a lot of uh rebels as you know 32  votes against two tellers last time  uh as well um if there may be more this  time not least from uh graham brady who  is the chairman of the backbench 1922  committee as many of my viewers  uh will know um if there are more  and labor votes against you or abstain  you're not going to get the easter  anyway  well look we have a job to do to speak  to and convince our parliamentary  colleagues  it doesn't surprise me that conservative  mps are particularly  concerned about these measures  conservatives by nature  want to support people's individual  freedoms want to support small  businesses  so these measures are things we do with  a really heavy heart none of us  when the prime minister downwards want  to be doing this but we do think a  strong tiered approach which enables a  localized  proportionate way forward is infinitely  better than the alternative  which is to have further national  lockdowns and also the difference  between now and the last time we were  debating measures like this when  there was concern in parliament is that  i think there is now a clear way  forwards there is a clear exit strategy  for the country  we've got ever increasing good news on  the vaccine front both the international  ones and our own  homegrown vaccines as a result of that  the question is  how can we steer the country through the  months remaining  until the vaccine rolls out just taking  this i'll come to the vaccine in a  second if i may i just wanted to just  press  you on that point if you don't manage to  get it through parliament what are you  going to do  well we live in a parliamentary  democracy so so the task is to persuade  our parliamentary  parliamentary colleagues well we if we  don't have the votes in parliament then  you you can't implement the policy  that's the way our system  works but we will be uh well we will be  working with our parliamentary  colleagues to try to persuade them  you know the virtue of this system and i  think we'll succeed because  by doing this we can keep the virus  under control  whilst we're rolling out the vaccine and  that is the big prize isn't it  ensuring that there isn't another  national lockdown and that there's now  a fairly seamless process towards easter  time  when the country can return to normality  you won't be able to implement these  changes unless you take parliament with  you there's no other alternative  well we've committed to the being a vote  in parliament so that's exactly what  we're going to do  okay um you talked about the vaccine a  bit of concern about the oxford vaccine  this morning not least because of  america wanting other tests and some  confusion about how  effective it is depending on which age  group you're in where are we with that  are we concerned  um i don't think there's any reason to  be unduly concerned i'm not an expert in  in vaccines so you're probably better  off off asking others but we have now  formally written to the uh body that  will  independently assess the veracity and  the safety  of the vaccine to do their work they'll  do that in the  coming days or or weeks as quickly as  they're able to  come to a judgment and then it'll be at  that point that armed with their  approval that we'll be able to start  rolling it out so there's no concern  there's no reason to be concerned  well i haven't heard reasons to be  concerned but we have to let the  independent  evaluation happen now the chief medical  officer last night at the prime  minister's press conference was very  clear  that although he has seen much of the  evidence  the full weight of evidence will now sit  with the independent evaluation who will  do their own analysis  decide whether it's right to move  forward if it is then it will be rolled  out we're pegging  on that vaccine aren't we we really need  it to work you say mentioned it  yesterday you also said that  we shouldn't hug family members at  christmas  will you be hugging your family members  at christmas well we're still deciding  what we're going to do at christmas i've  been talking to my  parents who one of whom is in his 80s my  mum's a bit younger both have been  shielding at different times  and we're going to make a judgment in  the coming weeks as to what we want to  do  and i think that's what families across  the country will be doing there are  rules now  you know we're very pleased we've been  able to agree across united kingdom  uh legal framework but that's just the  baseline isn't it  each individual family will be weighing  up uh is this the right thing  for you and your family based on your  own circumstances  are you willing to take the you know the  modest risk that's involved  in order to bring family together and  there are lots of different options from  the whole family coming together you  know at home to perhaps  meeting up for lunch with the windows  open or going out for a walk on  christmas day  so there are different options available  and families who have to use their own  personal judgment either way the dogs  involved  absolutely you saw him with uh mr sooner  yesterday under pressure  you bought a dog during lockdown yep i  well just before lockdown actually i  know i i bowed to pressure from my kids  just before so uh perhaps rishi will  follow  so you think you would tell him to get  one i've definitely already recommended  we've both got three daughters and so  i've already recommended to his  daughters that they get one  okay it's great to talk to you thanks so  much

